
diff --> line by line
cmp  --> byte by byte


diff file1 file2   --> to know actual diff between 2 files


diff -u file1.txt file2.txt   --> Unified format


diff -qr dir1/ dir2/          -->  -q — Report only whether files differ, not their contents.  -r — Recursively compare subdirectories.
                              -->  Compare two directory backups, This reports which corresponding files differ and which files are present only in one directory.


diff -i file1.txt file2.txt   --> case insensitive


diff -w file1.txt file2.txt   --> ignore whitespaces


diff -y file1.txt file2.txt              --> Side-by-side comparison
diff -y -W 120 file1.txt file2.txt       --> For wide files, specify a width
 | — A line differs.
 < — A line exists only in the first file.
 > — A line exists only in the second file.


diff -c file1.txt file2.txt       --> It shows the changed lines along with surrounding lines, making it easier to understand where the files differ.


diff -r dir1 dir2   --> Compare directories recursively


===========================================================================


cmp file1 file2    --> it will give whether both files are same or not


cmp -l a.txt b.txt   --> Report all differences


cmp archive1.gz archive2.gz    --> 


cmp -s a.txt b.txt    --> Quiet comparison
echo $?

Exit status:
 0 — Files are identical.
 1 — Files differ.
 Greater than 1 — An error occurred, such as an inaccessible file.


if cmp -s a.txt b.txt; then
    echo "Files are identical"
else
    echo "Files differ or comparison failed"
fi


For production scripts
======================

cmp -s a.txt b.txt
status=$?

case "$status" in
    0) echo "Files are identical" ;;
    1) echo "Files differ" ;;
    *) echo "Comparison failed" ;;
esac



Compare two files in a script
=============================

#!/bin/bash

if cmp -s "$1" "$2"; then
    echo "Files are identical"
else
    status=$?
    if [ "$status" -eq 1 ]; then
        echo "Files differ"
    else
        echo "Error comparing files"
        exit "$status"
    fi
fi

#save it as compare.sh
bash compare.sh file1.txt file2.txt








